Linux and Shell Scripting – (240) Interview Questions with Answers
Part 1: Linux Fundamentals and Architecture (Questions 1–40)
1. Explain Linux architecture with a diagram.
Answer:
Linux architecture is the structured in layers, that explains how users interact with hardware through different software layers.
Linux architecture has five major components:
1.	Hardware – CPU, RAM, hard disk, keyboard, network card.
2.	Kernel – manages hardware, memory, processes and devices.
3.	Shell – acts as an interface between users and the kernel.
4.	System utilities – commands such as ls, cp, mv, grep.
5.	Applications – browsers, databases, Tomcat, Jenkins, etc.
Linux architecture diagram:
USER / ADMINISTRATOR
Interacts with the Linux system
APPLICATIONS
Jenkins | Tomcat | MySQL | Browser
SHELL AND SYSTEM UTILITIES
Bash | ls | cp | grep | systemctl
KERNEL
Process | Memory | Device | File system | Network management
HARDWARE
CPU | RAM | HDD/SSD | Network devices
Interview answer: Linux architecture consists of hardware, kernel, shell, system utilities and applications. The kernel is the core component that manages system resources and communicates with hardware.
2. What is systemd?
Answer:
systemd is a system and service manager in Linux. It is responsible for starting and managing services during system boot.
Why do we use it?
•	To start services. To stop services. To restart services.To check service status. To manage the boot process.
Commands:
systemctl start nginx,systemctl stop nginx,systemctl restart nginx,systemctl status nginx
systemctl enable nginx,systemctl disable nginx
Example:
If Tomcat is stopped, we can start it using:
sudo systemctl start tomcat
Note: systemd is the service manager; systemctl is the command used to communicate with it.
3. What is LVM? What are its components?
Answer:
LVM stands for Logical Volume Manager.
It is used to manage disk storage flexibly. We can increase or decrease logical volumes without managing every physical disk partition manually.
Main components:
1.	PV – Physical Volume: Actual disk or partition.
2.	VG – Volume Group: Collection of physical volumes.
3.	LV – Logical Volume: Storage space created from a volume group.
 
Example commands:
pvcreate /dev/sdb
vgcreate vg_data /dev/sdb
lvcreate -L 5G -n lv_data vg_data
Check the details:
pvs
vgs
lvs
Real-time example:
Suppose your server has a 100 GB disk. Your application requires more storage. Using LVM, you can add another disk to the volume group and extend the logical volume.
4. What is the difference between TCP and UDP?
Answer:
TCP stands for Transmission Control Protocol.
UDP stands for User Datagram Protocol.
TCP	UDP
Connection-oriented	Connectionless
Reliable communication	No delivery guarantee
Ensures data delivery and order	No guarantee of delivery or order
Generally slower	Generally faster
Uses acknowledgements	Does not use acknowledgements
Used in SSH, HTTPS	Used in DNS, streaming, VoIP
Example:
•	TCP: Downloading a file. We need the complete file without missing data.
•	UDP: Live video or voice calls. Speed and low latency are important.
Interview answer: TCP provides reliable, ordered communication, whereas UDP provides faster, connectionless communication without guaranteed delivery.
5. What is the purpose of private IP and public IP? Give use cases.
Answer:
An IP address is a unique address used to identify a device on a network.
Private IP:
•	Used for internal communication.
•	Not directly accessible from the public internet.
•	Used inside an office network or AWS VPC.
Examples: 192.168.1.20
Public IP:
•	Used for communication over the internet.
•	Can be reachable from outside, subject to firewall and routing rules.
•	Used for publicly accessible websites and servers.
Real-time AWS example:
Suppose we launch two EC2 instances:
•	Web server: Public IP to receive internet traffic.
•	Database server: Private IP to communicate internally with the web server.
 
Interview answer: Private IPs are used for internal communication, while public IPs are used for internet-facing communication.
6. What is GRUB?
Answer:
GRUB stands for Grand Unified Bootloader.
It is a bootloader that helps load the Linux operating system into memory when the computer starts.
Boot process:
1.	BIOS/UEFI starts.
2.	GRUB loads.
3.	GRUB loads the Linux kernel.
4.	Kernel initializes hardware.
5.	systemd starts.
6.	Services start.
Important configuration files:
/etc/default/grub
/boot/grub2/grub.cfg
On some distributions, the GRUB configuration path is different.
Interview answer: GRUB is a bootloader responsible for loading the Linux kernel during system startup.
7. What is traceroute?
Answer:
traceroute is a networking command used to identify the network path taken by packets from the source system to the destination.
Why do we use it?
•	To troubleshoot network connectivity.
•	To identify where packets are getting delayed.
•	To identify intermediate routers.
•	To investigate network routing problems.
Command:
traceroute google.com
On some Linux systems:
tracepath google.com
Install if required:
sudo apt install traceroute
Example:
If your server cannot reach Google, traceroute helps identify the intermediate network hops and where the problem may be occurring.
8. What is a port? What commands are associated with it? What are listening and established ports?
Answer:
A port is a logical communication endpoint used by applications and services to communicate over a network.
Examples:
•	SSH – 22
•	HTTP – 80
•	HTTPS – 443
•	Jenkins – 8080 (default)
•	DNS – 53
Important commands:
ss -tulnp
netstat -tulnp
lsof -i :8080
nc -zv localhost 8080
Listening port:
A service is waiting for incoming connections.
Example:
0.0.0.0:8080 LISTEN
Established port:
An active connection has been established between two endpoints.
Example:
ESTAB
Difference:
LISTEN	ESTABLISHED
Waiting for connections	Active connection exists
Server is ready to accept connections	Communication is in progress
Example: SSH listening on port 22	User connected through SSH
9. What is strace? Where do we use it?
Answer:
strace is a Linux troubleshooting tool used to trace system calls made by a process.
It helps identify why an application or service is failing.
Why do we use it?
•	To troubleshoot application failures.
•	To identify missing files.
•	To identify permission problems.
•	To identify system call errors.
•	To troubleshoot application startup issues.
Example:
strace ls
Trace a running process:
strace -p 1234
Trace a command and save output:
strace -o output.log ls
Interview answer: strace helps troubleshoot applications by showing the system calls they execute and the errors they encounter.
10. What is nohup? Why do we use it?
Answer:
nohup stands for No Hang Up.
It allows a command to continue running even after the terminal is closed or the user disconnects.
Example:
nohup sh script.sh &
Save output in a specific file:
nohup sh script.sh > output.log 2>&1 &
Why do we use it?
Suppose you are running a backup script that takes two hours. If your SSH session disconnects, nohup helps the script continue running.
11. What are positional parameters? Why do we use them?
Answer:
Positional parameters are variables used to receive arguments passed to a shell script.
Example:
Create a script:
#!/bin/bash

echo "First number: $1"
echo "Second number: $2"
Execute:
bash test.sh 10 20
Output:
First number: 10
Second number: 20
Important positional parameters:
Variable	Meaning
$0	Script name
$1	First argument
$2	Second argument
$3	Third argument
$#	Number of arguments
$@	All arguments separately
$*	All arguments as a single string (when quoted)
Purpose: We use positional parameters to pass dynamic values to scripts instead of hardcoding them.
