39. What is redirect and append in Linux? Give the difference.
Answer:
Redirection is used to send command output to a file instead of displaying it on the terminal.
1. > – Redirect / overwrite
It replaces the existing file contents.
echo "Linux" > file.txt
2. >> – Append
It adds new content at the end of an existing file without deleting previous content.
echo "DevOps" >> file.txt
Difference:
>	>>
Overwrites existing content	Adds content at the end
Creates file if absent	Creates file if absent
Old content is removed	Old content remains
Other useful redirection operators:
command < input.txt
command 2> error.log
command > output.log 2>&1
40. Why do we use tee and tac commands?
Answer:
Tee command:
The tee command reads input and sends it to both the terminal and a file.
Example:
ls -l | tee output.txt
This displays the output on the terminal and saves it in output.txt.
Append mode:
ls -l | tee -a output.txt
TAC command:
The tac command displays file lines in reverse order.
Example:
tac file.txt
Suppose the file contains:
Linux
AWS
DevOps
Output:
DevOps
AWS
Linux
Difference:
tee	tac
Displays and saves command output	Displays lines in reverse order
Used for logging output	Used for reverse file viewing
Supports append with -a	Reads from end to beginning
